Z||000003||artherd||08-23-2001||02:09 PM||||HUNH?!<P>I just don't have an adequate responce here...  <P>I leave it to the reader as an excercise as to wether it is faster...  sheesh.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by falconhulk:<BR><B>I looked at that car yesterday..........the HP does not look like it would offset the weight of the motor.  I dont think that thing would be any faster then a 2.8 v6<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000004||mrfixit58||08-23-2001||02:16 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by falconhulk:<BR><B>.the HP does not look like it would offset the weight of the motor.  I dont think that thing would be any faster then a 2.8 v6</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Huh?!?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"> Is right! First off, the Caddy motor he's has in an aluminum block, aluminum intake, cast iron head motor. It weighs only 10 pounds more than the 2.8. Second, show me a 2.8 that has 275ft/lbs of torque that not on a bottle fed diet. The only thing that would make this slower than a 2.8 Fiero is traction problems.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>True, you can make a Fiero faster but it'll take more than a 2.8 to do it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>Roy :D<BR>87 GT   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG"> ||||reg||4
Z||000005||falconhulk||08-23-2001||03:46 PM||||really?  I thought the 4.5 liter was only 190 hp?  Sorry bout that if I screwed up.||||reg||1
Z||000006||mrfixit58||08-23-2001||04:16 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by falconhulk:<BR><B>really?  I thought the 4.5 liter was only 190 hp?  Sorry bout that if I screwed up.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Horsepower is a simple mathimatical calculation of the torque and RPM. If you see a large torque number and a small horsepower number, that means the motor makes most of it's power at low rpms (e.g., the 4.1, 4.5, & 4.9L Caddys). <P>Conversely, if you see a low torque number and a high HP, most of the motor's power is at the higher RPMs (e.g., DOHC motors like the Northstar, SHOs, etc). No need to apologize, were all frends here.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>"Horsepower is for show, torque is for GO!"<P>------------------<BR>Roy :D<BR>87 GT   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||rockcrawl||08-23-2001||04:41 PM||||Actually the '89 4.5 is only rated at 155hp, but it's still got a lot more torque and only a negligible weight increase over a 2.8. And the V8 rumble. I'm going to weigh a 4.5 tonight just to find out the actual weight of it. I wish I had a 2.8 around to compare.||||reg||1
Z||000008||banditbalz||08-23-2001||04:47 PM||||Mr.Fixit has it right. There are only 2 ways of making more hp. Eithor get the motor spinning faster or give it more torque. There's only 3 equations in the formula. hp (max rpm) x torque output (173lb.ft) divided by 5252 = horsepower. example, a stock 2.8 builds it's peak hp around 4500. multiply this number by 173 (max. torque) and divide by 5252. I read this formula in HotRod magazine years ago and it works with all Internal combustion engines. <P>Do the math...<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sunset.jpg"> <P><P>------------------<BR>Silver 86 GT<BR>completely stock,<BR>completely fast!||||reg||1
Z||000009||mrfixit58||08-23-2001||05:30 PM||||Let's see... 5 gizenta 7, carry the 2... 148hp (max) for 2.8L.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>Roy :D<BR>87 GT   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG"> ||||reg||3
